        "blast_url": "https://blast.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/Blast.cgi?PAGE_TYPE=BlastSearch&PROG_DEF=blastn&BLAST_SPEC=GDH_GCA_965648485.1",
        "comments": "The assembly drSorBris1.hap1.1 is based on 49x PacBio data and Arima2 Hi-C data generated by the Darwin Tree of Life Project\n(https://www.darwintreeoflife.org/). The assembly process included the following sequence of steps: initial PacBio assembly generation with Hifiasm in Hi-C integrated assembly mode, and Hi-C based scaffolding with YaHS. The mitochondrial and chloroplast genomes were assembled using OATK. Finally, each haplotype assembly was analysed and manually improved using TreeVal. Chromosome-scale scaffolds confirmed by the Hi-C data have been named in order of size. This species appears to be allotriploid based on kmer spectra and contact patterns in the merged (all haplotypes) HIC map. Complex patterns of hybridisation and polyploidy have been observed in the genus (https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1365-294X.2010.04585.x). Based on HIC signal in the merged map, two haplotypes are highly similar to each other and the third haplotype is more divergent, suggesting two sub-genomes corresponding to the parental species. The primary (HAP1) assembly contains 34 chromosomes corresponding to 1 copy of each sub genome (A and B). The alternate assembly (HAP2) contains 17 chromosomes (C) which are highly similar to the A subgenome. Chromosome 3 appears to have resolved the ancestral hybridisation event differently to the other sets of chromosomes and / or undergone rearrangement between homeologs. The first\n22 Mb of the C copy (including the centromere region) is highly similar to the B copy whereas the region of the C copy from\n22 Mb to the end is highly similar to the A copy.",
